// `@T|m` inside a generic function `<T|m>` body, with `witness:T[m]` as
// the inference source for T and m. The cast should dispatch to the
// `cast_Any_MANY__…` variant (signature 3 — both T and m from the holder),
// NOT `cast_Any_m__…` (signature 1 — m inferred from source) which would
// silently discard the user's `m` constraint.
//
// This was broken until `_Multiplicity.subsumes` was fixed to reject the
// `?` (UndefinedMultiplicity) vs `m` (MultiplicityParameter) match — they
// are semantically distinct (user-omitted vs user-provided) and shouldn't
// unify, which is what was causing the resolver to accept signature 1.

###Pure
function test::run<T|m>(witness:T[m], probe:Any[*]):T[m]
{
   $probe->cast(@T|m)
}

